This creates a race condition in the parallel reduction. Without the synchronization barrier, threads can read stale values from shared memory before other threads have finished writing their updates. The bug manifests as a warp-level race condition—while threads within a warp execute in lockstep and don't need explicit synchronization, threads across different warps do. When `s >= 32`, threads from different warps access overlapping memory locations without coordination, leading to data races and incorrect reduction results. read from threads 128-255, and so on down to s=16 and s=8 where operations stay within individual warps. The critical issue emerges at s=512 when threads 0-511 attempt to read values that threads 512-1023 must first write—this cross-warp dependency lacks synchronization, so threads 0-511 could read stale or uninitialized data from shared memory before the other warps complete their writes.

However, examining the actual execution flow reveals the first loop iteration (s=512) has threads 512-1023 sitting idle since their thread IDs exceed 512, meaning they never execute the addition. The real sequence is: all threads initially write their local_sum to shared_mem[tid], then __syncthreads() ensures visibility, then the loop begins with threads 0-511 performing shared_mem[tid] += shared_mem[tid+512] while threads 512-1023 remain inactive. Without syncthreads between iterations, threads 0-255 could read from shared_mem[256-511] before threads 0-255 finish their s=512 writes. Threads 256-511 wrote their results to shared_mem[256-511] during the first iteration, and threads 0-255 need to read those values in the second iteration. Without a synchronization barrier, threads 0-255 could advance to iteration 2 before threads 256-511 finish writing, causing a race condition.

For this bug to manifest visibly, the values at positions 256-511 must actually change between iterations, and the scheduler must allow the earlier threads to pull ahead. When all input values are uniform (like 1.0), every thread processes the same amount of work due to the stride pattern, so the intermediate results remain identical across iterations—masking the synchronization error even though it technically exists. race condition due to how the GPU schedules execution.

